{"id": "2a2f1bf3-6181-4500-ac6b-b29d7b6f058b", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T02:19:27.045922", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "b72f8909-6e66-493e-b750-4b857547ac40", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "The reviewer approved and is asking for a full experimental design at stage=results. I need to write a comprehensive design-only document covering 5 sections, and I have to use [UNVERIFIED] tags on library symbols and a source-verification checklist. Before writing, it might be good to do a quick check of the environment one more time (already done). I could also double-check the availability of numpy/scipy for the fallback — pip list showed a lot of packages. numpy wasn't in the top 40. Let me check whether numpy/scipy are importable (for the fallback reference implementation). Actually, the earlier output of pip3 list | head -40 got cut off at J…. Let me quickly check for numpy/scipy.\n\nAfter that, I'll create the full design. It should be thorough. Let me carefully draft it.\n\nKey design decisions:\n\n**Problem setup:**\n- Shared data: ridge-type inner problem per meta-config. Task: given A ∈ R^{m×d}, b(φ) = B φ (or b depends on meta-parameter φ through a learned linear map), inner problem:\n  min_x f(x; φ) = 1/2 ‖A x − b(φ)‖² + λ/2 ‖x‖², λ > 0 → strongly convex, Hessian H = AᵀA + λI ≻ 0, unique root x*(φ) = H⁻¹ Aᵀ b(φ). Conditioning κ = (σmax² + λ)/(σmin² + λ).\n- Choice of where φ enters: phi enters the inner problem **through the objective's data term b(φ)** (regularizer/initialization only as ablation variants). Rationale: IFT well-posedness — by the implicit function theorem, we need ∇ₓf(x*(φ), φ)=0 and non-singularity of ∂∇ₓf/∂x = H(φ) (which doesn't depend on φ here → uniform non-singularity). If φ were only in the initialization, the inner root wouldn't depend on φ at all → meta-gradient is zero, IFT is vacuous; that's the stop-gradient-like degenerate case. Also, variant where φ is a meta-learned initialization x₀(φ) — in that case, the inner problem min_x 1/2‖Ax−b‖² + λ/2‖x − φ‖² gives x*(φ) = H⁻¹(Aᵀb + λφ) — also well-posed and useful as a second variant. Can be kept as a secondary variant. Main axis: data-embedding φ via b(φ) = U φ (φ ∈ R^{d×k} or a small MLP).\n- Meta-objective: g(x*(φ); φ) = 1/2 ‖C x*(φ) − y‖² (meta-loss on meta-validation mapping), optionally a direct φ term. Meta-gradient via chain rule through x*.\n- Inner solver: fixed-point / gradient map with step size η: T(x; φ) = x − η∇ₓf(x; φ). Residual criterion: ‖∇ₓf(x_t; φ)‖ ≤ tol · ‖∇ₓf(x₀; φ)‖ or absolute tol. Inner solve error ε acts as a bias on the IFT gradient: reported relative error floor ~ O(ε · condition number stuff) — must be measured as a separate curve (sweep tol over 1e-4..1e-10) and kept separate from truncation bias.\n\n**Formulations:**\n(a) Fixed-point form: x* = T(x*; φ). IFT: dx*/dφ v = ∂T/∂x dx*/dφ v + ∂T/∂φ v → (I − J_x T) dx*/dφ = ∂T/∂φ. Vector-Jacobian product for backward: (I − J_x Tᵀ) u = g_x, i.e., solve u − J_x Tᵀ u = g_x; J_x T = I − ηH → system is ηH u = g_x → equivalent to solving H u = g_x. Required operator products: per iteration, H v via double-backprop jvp (∇ₓ(∇f·v)), i.e., R-operator; and transpose side. Solver: CG on SPD H (since H ⪰ λI), stopping criterion ‖residual‖ ≤ tol_inner_lin. Convergence condition: fixed-point *iteration* converges when ‖I − ηH‖ < 1, i.e., η < 2/λmax(H); non-singularity of (I − J_xT) = ηH requires only η ≠ 1/λᵢ(H). Non-convergence handling: if ρ = max(|1−ηλmin|, |1−ηλmax|) ≥ 1 the iteration diverges; design: compute/estimate λmax via power iteration (cheap) and set η = 1/λ̂max (guarantees ρ ≤ (κ−1)/(κ+1)); additionally, provide Anderson acceleration [UNVERIFIED: torchopt.accelerate.anderson] as a rescue for near-violations; document the fallback: switch to formulation (b) (which doesn't require convergence, only non-singularity) when η is in the unsafe band — this is the substantive rationale: (a) requires both convergence of the forward iteration and non-singularity; (b) requires only non-singularity of the Jacobian of the residual map.\n(b) Root-of-optimality form: F(x; φ) = ∇ₓf(x; φ) = 0. IFT: J_x F dx*/dφ = −∂F/∂φ, J_x F = H(φ) is SPD. Backward: solve H u = g_x via CG; Hessian-vector products via autodiff. Forward solve via any method (can use torchopt optimizers as pseudo-dynamical systems; e.g., gradient descent, or LBFGS [UNVERIFIED]). Newton-CG is second-order; Hvp cost is dominated by the number of gradient evaluations. Also mention non-symmetric/ill-conditioned variants in the stress arm: replace H with AᵀA + λI with tiny λ → CG convergence slows; stopping criterion is relative residual reduction; record number of CG iterations.\n\n**Arms:**\n- A1: TorchOpt fixed_point_iteration [UNVERIFIED] (φ inside b(φ)).\n- A2: TorchOpt root/zero residual version [UNVERIFIED; may not exist — fallback: use fixed_point_iteration with a gradient-map T which is the same math].\n- B: K sweep 1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50, 100, 200 unrolled, create_graph=True.\n- C: K=2000 long unroll, double backprop through the full trajectory (memory-heavy — chunking; CPU-fine if d≤300).\n- D: Analytical: x*(φ)=H⁻¹Aᵀb(φ), exact meta-gradient via explicit solves (torch.linalg.solve with autograd through solve, or explicit matrix calculus); FD: forward + central difference step-size sweep h ∈ {1e-1…1e-8} log-spaced, complex-step if all ops are analytic (pure matmul + solve → complex-step works in torch: torch supports complex; but does linalg.solve support complex? It does. So complex-step h=1e-20 gives machine-precision reference); FD band rule: treat central-difference plateau as ground truth, accept |central − complex| and |analytic − complex| < 1e-7 relative.\n- E (ablation): stop-gradient variant of B with K ∈ {5, 50}: gradient is zero / random walk — shows how much of the small-K meta-loss decrease is actual signal.\n- F (stress): condition number κ ~ 1e6 by setting λ ∈ {1e-6, ...}; A/B degrade differently; report where A's IFT gradient error explodes (CG solve failure vs. inner tolerance).\n\n**Metrics / analysis:**\n- Error norm: relative error ||ĝ − g_D||_2 / ||g_D||_2, g_D is analytical (complex-step verified); report per-parameter-tensor and flattened.\n- Compute axes: wall time (median over 20 repetitions), count of first-order gradient evaluations (∇f oracle calls) counted explicitly by a wrapper.\n- Memory: torch.cuda not available — use tracetool `torch.profiler` or peak Python `tracemalloc` / `resource.getrusage` RSS delta.\n- Seeds: 10 seeds for problem-instance draws (A, b, φ₀); report median and IQR across seeds; per-arm paired comparison at matched compute.\n- Meaningful difference: paired differences across seeds; treat as meaningful if the 95% bootstrap CI on the median relative-error difference excludes zero AND the effect exceeds a noise floor established by a null experiment (gradient of a φ-independent loss: expected error ≈ 0 but numerical floor is ~1e-7; use max(null error, 1e-6) as the noise floor).\n- Decision criteria: hypothesis \"implicit achieves reference-level accuracy at a fraction of compute/memory while decoupling from inner-loop length\" is:\n  - Supported if: median relative error of A < 2× noise floor while B requires K ≥ K*, with compute(A) ≤ 0.3 × compute(B at K*) to achieve relative error ≤ 2× that floor, across ≥8/10 seeds; and wall/memory of A is flat with respect to inner tolerance tightening vs. B/C cost growing linearly in K.\n  - Falsified if: A's error floor ≥ ½ of B's error at matched compute, or A doesn't beat K=50 within 2× compute, or A fails in the well-conditioned regime (indicating a setup/usage error → design instructs to fall back to reference implementation).\n  - Ill-conditioned stress arm is reported separately — the outcome there doesn't falsify the main claim but bounds its scope.\n\n**Feasibility:**\n- Dimensions: m=500, d=200, φ ∈ R^{d×k}, k=10 meta-inputs; H is 200×200 → direct solve is trivial; CG is fast. Per-arm cost: A: inner solve ~ few×1e3 gradient evals, CG ≤ 200 iterations per meta-step; B: K gradient evals + autodag overhead; C: 2000 evals, double backprop through the graph, estimated memory ~ K × d × 8B ≈ 3.2MB trajectory — trivial. Total sweep < a few minutes per seed; 10 seeds × arms ≪ 1 hour on a single core; fits comfortably within CPU budget.\n- Python 3.14 install risk: torch may not have cp314 wheels; network is disabled anyway. Fallback: reference implementation that still answers the question — implement the same API surface in pure PyTorch (or numpy/scipy if torch is unavailable — check numpy availability; from earlier pip list I recall numpy wasn't in the first 40 lines but the output was cut off; quick check needed). The fallback reference: `fixed_point_ift(fn, x0, ...)`, a custom autograd Function with CG backward, structurally mirroring the API-level design; comparison with library becomes a plug-in swap.\n\n**Source-verification checklist** mapping [UNVERIFIED] symbols to grep targets:\n- `torchopt.diff.implicit.fixed_point_iteration` → `grep -rn \"def fixed_point_iteration\" torchopt/diff/implicit/`; docs: docs/... implicit differentiation guide.\n- `solve_implicit` / `zero_root` / `root_finding` → `grep -rn \"def .*root\\|def solve\" torchopt/diff/`\n- `torchopt.CGD` / inner optimizer for linear solve → `ls torchopt/linear_solver/` (I recall torchopt has a linear_solver module with HessianCG, conjugate_gradient) grep \"class .*CG\".\n- `torchopt.accelerate.anderson` → `ls torchopt/accelerate/`\n- Functional optimizer API `torchopt.SGD/Adam` update/step state API → `torchopt/optim/`, examples/\n- `torchopt.meta` for meta-learned initialization → `ls torchopt/meta/`\n- Examples: `ls examples/` look for fixed-point / inner-loop examples; tests: `ls tests/` grep \"implicit\".\n- Anderson/CG tolerance semantics → docstrings.\n\nLet me quickly check numpy/scipy availability for the feasibility section, then submit the results.\n\nTwo quick checks to back up the feasibility section (whether a fallback via numpy/scipy